                "content": "\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\bigl(x,y,z,w\\bigr)=\\Bigl(\\tan \\frac{k\\pi}{15},\n        \\tan \\frac{2k\\pi}{15},\n        \\tan \\frac{4k\\pi}{15},\n        \\tan \\frac{8k\\pi}{15}\\Bigr),\n        \\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Why this description is complete  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nf(t)=\\frac{2t}{1-t^{2}}\\qquad (t\\in\\mathbb C,\\;1-t^{2}\\neq0).\n\\]\n\nThe system can be written compactly as  \n\n\\[\ny=f(x),\\quad z=f(y),\\quad w=f(z),\\quad x=f(w),\n\\]\nor equivalently  \n\n\\[\nf\\!\\bigl(f\\!\\bigl(f\\!\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)\\bigr)\\bigr)=x .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A trigonometric interpretation  \n\nRecall the double\u2011angle identity for the tangent:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(2\\theta)=\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1-\\tan^{2}\\theta}.\n\\]\n\nHence, if we put  \n\n\\[\nx=\\tan\\theta ,\n\\]\n\nthen  \n\n\\[\ny=f(x)=\\tan(2\\theta),\\qquad \nz=f(y)=\\tan(4\\theta),\\qquad \nw=f(z)=\\tan(8\\theta).\n\\]\n\nCondition \\(x=f(w)\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\tan\\theta=\\tan(16\\theta).\n\\]\n\nTwo tangents are equal iff their arguments differ by an integer multiple of \\(\\pi\\):\n\\[\n16\\theta-\\theta=k\\pi\\qquad(k\\in\\mathbb Z)\n\\Longrightarrow\\;15\\theta=k\\pi .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\theta=\\frac{k\\pi}{15},\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Obtaining the quadruple  \n\nSubstituting \\(\\theta=k\\pi/15\\) into the expressions above gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\tan\\!\\left(\\frac{k\\pi}{15}\\right),\\\\[2mm]\ny&=\\tan\\!\\left(\\frac{2k\\pi}{15}\\right),\\\\[2mm]\nz&=\\tan\\!\\left(\\frac{4k\\pi}{15}\\right),\\\\[2mm]\nw&=\\tan\\!\\left(\\frac{8k\\pi}{15}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\tan(\\theta+\\pi)=\\tan\\theta\\), different integers \\(k\\) that differ by a multiple of \\(15\\) give the same quadruple.  \nConsequently it suffices to let \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,14\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  No other solutions  \n\nIf a solution \\((x,y,z,w)\\) satisfies the system, then \\(x\\) belongs to the orbit of the rational map \\(f\\) under iteration.  \nEquation (1) says that \\(x\\) lies in the **fourth iterate** of \\(f\\).  \nAll points of the fourth iterate are exactly the points obtained by starting from a value \\(\\tan\\theta\\) and applying \\(f\\) four times, i.e. the points described above.  \n\nOne must also check that none of the intermediate denominators vanish:\n\\(1-x^{2},\\,1-y^{2},\\,1-z^{2},\\,1-w^{2}\\neq0\\).  \nFor \\(\\theta=k\\pi/15\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\cos\\theta\\neq0,\\;\\cos2\\theta\\neq0,\\;\\cos4\\theta\\neq0,\\;\\cos8\\theta\\neq0,\n\\]\n\nso all denominators stay non\u2011zero. Hence every tuple produced above is admissible, and no other tuples exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer  \n\nAll solutions of  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2x}{1-x^{2}}=y,\\qquad\n\\frac{2y}{1-y^{2}}=z,\\qquad\n\\frac{2z}{1-z^{2}}=w,\\qquad\n\\frac{2w}{1-w^{2}}=x\n\\]\n\nwith \\((x,y,z,w)\\in\\mathbb C^{4}\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\bigl(x,y,z,w\\bigr)=\\Bigl(\\tan \\frac{k\\pi}{15},\n        \\tan \\frac{2k\\pi}{15},\n        \\tan \\frac{4k\\pi}{15},\n        \\tan \\frac{8k\\pi}{15}\\Bigr),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z }.\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, taking \\(k=0,1,\\ldots ,14\\) gives the fifteen distinct quadruples.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to solve system of equations over complex numbers.\n\nDefine function f(t) = 2t/(1 - t^2). Then we have y = f(x), z = f(y), w = f(z), and x = f(w). So composition: applying f four times returns original: f(f(f(f(x)))) = x.\n\nThus we need fixed points of the fourth iterate of f.\n\nLet\u2019s analyze f. It's rational map of degree 2 (since denominator quadratic). Compute its dynamics.\n\nFirst find fixed points of f: solutions of t = f(t) => t = 2t/(1 - t^2) => multiply both sides: t(1 - t^2) = 2t => if t=0 then left side zero? Let's compute: If t=0, RHS 0, LHS 0, works. For t \u2260 0 divide by t: 1 - t^2 = 2 => -t^2 = 1 => t^2 = -1 => t = i or -i. So fixed points: {0, i, -i}. Those satisfy f(t)=t.\n\nNow we need points where after 4 iterations return to start; could be cycles of length dividing 4 (including fixed points). So we need preimages under iteration.\n\nCompute f's action: maybe easier to consider that f is tangent double-angle formula: sin(2\u03b8)/cos(2\u03b8)? Actually tan double angle: tan(2\u03b8) = 2tan \u03b8 / (1 - tan^2 \u03b8). Indeed f(t) = tan(2\u03b8) when t = tan \u03b8. Good! So define mapping on angles: if x = tan \u03b8, then y = tan(2\u03b8), z = tan(4\u03b8), w = tan(8\u03b8), and condition x = tan(16\u03b8). So we need tan \u03b8 = tan(16\u03b8). Over complex numbers, tangent defined as sin/cos.\n\nEquation tan \u03b8 = tan(16\u03b8) implies 16\u03b8 - \u03b8 = k\u03c0 for integer k, i.e., 15\u03b8 = k\u03c0 => \u03b8 = k\u03c0/15. However also need to ensure denominators not zero: 1 - x^2 \u2260 0 etc. That corresponds to cos \u03b8 \u2260 0? Since tan \u03b8 = sin \u03b8 / cos \u03b8, denominator zero when cos \u03b8 = 0 => \u03b8 = \u03c0/2 + m\u03c0. But those would make x infinite? Not allowed because expression defined requires denominator nonzero. So exclude such.\n\nSimilarly need 1 - y^2 \u2260 0 etc. In terms of \u03b8, y = tan(2\u03b8); denominator zero when cos(2\u03b8)=0 => 2\u03b8 = \u03c0/2 + n\u03c0 => \u03b8 = \u03c0/4 + n\u03c0/2. Similarly for other steps.\n\nBut perhaps many solutions correspond to \u03b8 = k\u03c0/15 with restrictions.\n\nLet's compute x = tan \u03b8 = tan(k\u03c0/15). Need to check that none of intermediate values cause denominator zero: i.e., cos \u03b8 \u2260 0, cos(2\u03b8)\u22600, cos(4\u03b8)\u22600, cos(8\u03b8)\u22600. Equivalent to \u03b8 not equal to odd multiples of \u03c0/2, etc.\n\nGiven \u03b8 = k\u03c0/15, evaluate conditions:\n\n- cos \u03b8 = 0 when \u03b8 = \u03c0/2 + m\u03c0 => k\u03c0/15 = \u03c0/2 + m\u03c0 => k/15 = 1/2 + m => k = 7.5 + 15m -> not integer unless m half-integer. So no integer k gives cos \u03b8 =0. So fine.\n\n- cos(2\u03b8) = 0 when 2\u03b8 = \u03c0/2 + n\u03c0 => \u03b8 = \u03c0/4 + n\u03c0/2 => k\u03c0/15 = \u03c0/4 + n\u03c0/2 => k/15 = 1/4 + n/2 => k = 15/4 + (15n)/2 = 3.75 + 7.5 n. For integer k, need n such that RHS integer. Let n even? Let n = 2m => RHS = 3.75 + 15m = 3.75 + 15m. Not integer because .75 fraction. n odd? n=2m+1 => RHS = 3.75 + 7.5(2m+1) = 3.75 + 15m +7.5 = 11.25 +15m = 11.25 +15m not integer. So never integer. So cos(2\u03b8) never zero.\n\n- cos(4\u03b8) = 0 when 4\u03b8 = \u03c0/2 + p\u03c0 => \u03b8 = \u03c0/8 + p\u03c0/4 => k\u03c0/15 = \u03c0/8 + p\u03c0/4 => k/15 = 1/8 + p/4 => k = 15/8 + (15p)/4 = 1.875 + 3.75 p. For integer k, need p such that RHS integer. Try p even? p=2m => RHS =1.875 + 7.5 m = 1.875 +7.5m not integer. p odd? p=2m+1 => RHS =1.875 + 3.75(2m+1)=1.875+7.5m+3.75=5.625+7.5m not integer. So never integer.\n\n- cos(8\u03b8) = 0 when 8\u03b8 = \u03c0/2 + q\u03c0 => \u03b8 = \u03c0/16 + q\u03c0/8 => k\u03c0/15 = \u03c0/16 + q\u03c0/8 => k/15 = 1/16 + q/8 => k = 15/16 + (15q)/8 = 0.9375 + 1.875 q. For integer k, need q such that RHS integer. Try q even? q=2m => RHS =0.9375 + 3.75 m not integer. q odd? q=2m+1 => RHS =0.9375 + 1.875(2m+1)=0.9375+3.75m+1.875=2.8125+3.75m not integer. So never integer.\n\nThus all k produce valid solutions.\n\nTherefore solutions: x = tan(k\u03c0/15) for integer k, and then y = tan(2k\u03c0/15), z = tan(4k\u03c0/15), w = tan(8k\u03c0/15).\n\nBut note periodicity: tan has period \u03c0, so k modulo 15 yields same x? Because tan(\u03b8+\u03c0) = tan \u03b8, so k mod 15 gives same x. Also there may be duplicates due to symmetry: e.g., k and k+15 give same. So distinct solutions correspond to k = 0,...,14.\n\nCheck special cases: k=0 gives \u03b8=0 => x=0, y=0,... which matches fixed point.\n\nk=5 gives \u03b8=\u03c0/3 => tan \u03c0/3 = \u221a3, y = tan(2\u03c0/3) = tan(120\u00b0) = -\u221a3, z = tan(4\u03c0/3) = tan(240\u00b0) = \u221a3, w = tan(8\u03c0/3) = tan(8\u03c0/3 - 2\u03c0) = tan(2\u03c0/3) = -\u221a3, and x = tan(16\u03c0/3) = tan(16\u03c0/3 - 5*2\u03c0) = tan(\u03c0/3) = \u221a3. Works.\n\nThus total 15 solutions.\n\nBut also need to consider possibility of poles where denominator zero leading to infinity? The equation defined only where denominators non-zero. So we exclude those.\n\nHence answer: All quadruples given by (tan(k\u03c0/15), tan(2k\u03c0/15), tan(4k\u03c0/15), tan(8k\u03c0/15)), k\u2208\u2124.\n\nAlternatively express using complex exponentials: tan \u03b8 = (e^{i\u03b8}-e^{-i\u03b8})/(i(e^{i\u03b8}+e^{-i\u03b8})) = ... Could also write in terms of 2\u03b8 etc.\n\nProvide final answer.\n\nAlso verify that these indeed satisfy original equations: yes because they come from repeated application of f.\n\nThus final answer.\n\nPotentially also include solutions where some variable equals \u221e? Not allowed.\n\nThus final answer: 15 distinct solutions.\n\nWrite as set S = { (tan(k\u03c0/15), tan(2k\u03c0/15), tan(4k\u03c0/15), tan(8k\u03c0/15)) | k \u2208 \u2124 }.\n\nSimplify: Since tan periodic \u03c0, we can restrict k = 0,\u2026,14.\n\nReturn.\n\n"
